  "notes": "max_tokens is now validated on GenerateRequest and ChatRequest: <1 -> 422, >MAX_TOKENS_LIMIT (env, default 1024, non-numeric/<1 env falls back to 1024) -> 422 (reject, not clamp, so clients never silently get a shorter generation); default stays 64. What clients actually send: frontend/src never sends max_tokens (api/client.ts sendChat posts only conversation_id+message, so the UI always gets the default 64); bench/k6.js and bench/k6_chat.js send 64; bench/workload.json has no max_tokens; existing tests send 8 (/v1/generate) and 16 (/v1/chat); README did not mention it before. The router's request log (RequestLogMiddleware) logs method/path/status/duration only, not bodies, so bench logs cannot show values beyond the k6 scripts' 64. All in-repo clients are within 1..1024, so nothing legitimate is rejected. The mock backend's latency is independent of max_tokens (it also caps completions at 256 words itself), and the bench always sends 64, so no bench movement is expected: medians are within run-to-run noise. The value is protecting real backends from negative/zero/runaway generation requests, not latency.",
